{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>״Take my discipline rather than silver, and knowledge rather than choice gold״ (Proverbs 8:10).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, admonishes here (Proverbs 8) man regarding the trait of alacrity (zerizut), that he should strive and exert himself in the discipline (musar) of Torah and not strive and exert himself in amassing silver, because through the discipline of Torah a man acquires many benefits in this world and in the world to come, whereas the amassing of silver holds no benefit but rather contains many harms and stumbling-blocks, for it is the cause of many worries, as the Sages, blessed be their memory, said: ״He who increases possessions increases worry.״ And it is known that among all the multitudes of all traits there is no benefit whatsoever except in the multiplicity of Torah. ״And knowledge rather than choice gold״ — its meaning is: acquire the knowledge of Torah, which is choice gold; and he used the expression ״take״ to say that one should take the discipline of Torah and the commandments with alacrity and striving, just as one takes the matter with both hands, that is, acts zealously in it, and not take them in laziness and despair, for if one is lazy in them it is impossible to escape punishment even when he fulfills the commandment, for if he designates the Holy One, blessed be He, with his mouth and lips while his heart is far from Him, his iniquity is too great to bear, for from one side he performs a commandment and from another side he is punished in performing it, when he did not perform it in perfection but rather in laziness. And the same judgment and reasoning apply to the matter of prayer: if he prayed and did not direct his heart in his prayer but instead thinks about the vanities of the time — about his merchandise, his silver, and his gold — behold, this one is a sinner, and it is almost as if concerning him it is said (Exodus 20:20): ״You shall not make alongside Me gods of silver and gods of gold,״ that is to say: when you are with Me and standing in prayer before El, do not think about the silver and gold that are with you, for the one who prays and does this dissembles in his thought toward the King, the Holy One, blessed be He — and before a king of flesh and blood he would not do so. And likewise, if one laid tefillin and was lazy and did not take care not to sleep in them and not to puff air into them, the commandment is not complete, and so too all the commandments. From this you have learned that the sluggard, even in performing one of the commandments, incurs sin and iniquity on account of his laziness. Now, the benefit he attained in fulfilling the commandment is not without punishment, just as the benefit of vinegar that a person sometimes drinks for medicine is not without harm — for it is to the teeth — or the benefit of the heat of fire, which is not without the smoke that harms the eyes; and concerning this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 10:26): ״As vinegar to the teeth and as smoke to the eyes, so is the sluggard to those who send him.״ Therefore Solomon always condemned the trait of the sluggard, which is what he said (Proverbs 13:4): ״The soul of the sluggard desires and has nothing״ — he means that anyone whose soul desires something, whatever it may be, and he has nothing because he did not attain it — he is a sluggard, because he did not exert himself in it sufficiently, as the Sages, blessed be their memory, expounded: ״I toiled and found — believe; I did not toil and found — do not believe; I toiled and did not find — do not believe.״ And therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, continually presses his admonition against the sluggard and directs us to the ant, which is what he said (Proverbs 6): ״Go to the ant, O sluggard; consider her ways and be wise,״ for from her we may derive an a fortiori argument (kal va-ḥomer), and in her one may see a matter of wisdom, for she is the lowliest of creeping things, yet she possesses the trait of alacrity: she gathers during the summer what she will eat during the winter." "<b>And it is known</b> that one who is zealous in the matters of Torah and commandments and also zealous in the rectification of his traits — behold, he ascends from level to level and from rung to rung, going forward all day long and not backward. Now, the sluggard is the opposite of him, for he goes backward all day long and not forward, which is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 15:19): ״The way of the sluggard is like a hedge of thorns,״ for it is impossible for him to walk and pass to the place his heart desires, and he must turn back. So too the sluggard cannot ascend from level to level, but rather he destroys himself inside and out, for he lays waste to his soul and his body — like the likeness of a field that is desolate within, where under the wheat thorns and thistles sprout, and without, for the walls and fences that surrounded it are all in ruins. And concerning this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 24:30–31): ״I passed by the field of a sluggard… and behold, it was all overgrown with thorns.״ He likened the sluggard who does not rectify his soul and his traits, and who through his evil conduct destroys his soul and body inside and out, to the sluggard who refrains from working his field and leaves it to be laid waste inside and out. And therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, mentioned here: ״Take my discipline rather than silver,״ that creatures should take the discipline of Torah with alacrity and not with laxness and sloth. And he said ״rather than silver״ in contrast to the multitude of the people, who strive to acquire silver with alacrity and diligence — and this is their way of folly — just as they do not do with the discipline of the commandment. Therefore he said: ״Take my discipline rather than silver,״ that is, the Torah, for the Torah is called discipline (musar), as it is written (Proverbs 6:23): ״And the way of life, reproofs and discipline (musar).״ And through it a man merits eternal life, to take pleasure in the splendor of the Shekhinah, for the Shekhinah cleaves to the Torah. And this is what the Sages, blessed be their memory, expounded on (Proverbs 2:21): ״For the upright shall dwell in the land״ — ישכינו שכינה בארץ, ״they shall cause the Shekhinah to dwell in the land.״ At the giving of the Torah it is written (Exodus 24:16): ״And the glory of HaShem dwelt upon Mount Sinai,״ for it [the Torah] is the cause of the Shekhinah dwelling in Israel — at Mount Sinai in the revealed, and in the Tabernacle in the concealed. At Mount Sinai in the revealed: this is that the glory dwelt upon Mount Sinai before the eyes of all Israel, as it is said (Exodus 24:17): ״And the appearance of the glory of HaShem was like devouring fire on the top of the mount before the eyes of the children of Israel.״ In the Tabernacle in the concealed: this is that we were commanded to make for Him a designated house, so that He would dwell upon it in the concealed. And on this account Israel volunteered a terumah to make the Tabernacle from it, and this is what is written:" "<b>And they shall take for Me a terumah, from every man whose heart moves him you shall take My terumah.</b> ״And they shall take for Me״ — by way of the plain sense (peshat): for My Name; and the expression of terumah is separation, meaning that Israel shall set aside a voluntary gift from their property for the work of the Tabernacle. And the significance of the Tabernacle was a parallel to Mount Sinai; and the glory that dwelt upon Mount Sinai in the revealed is what dwelt upon the Tabernacle in the concealed. And just as we find at Mount Sinai that Scripture says (Deuteronomy 5:21): ״HaShem our Elohim has shown us His glory and His greatness,״ so too we find regarding the Tabernacle, where it repeats twice (Exodus 40:34–35): ״And the glory of HaShem filled the Tabernacle.״ And just as we find at Mount Sinai that it is written concerning it (Deuteronomy 4:36): ״From heaven He made you hear His voice, to discipline you; and upon earth He showed you His great fire,״ and it is written (ibid.): ״And His words you heard from the midst of the fire״ — so too in the Tabernacle the voice would speak to him from above the kapporet, from between the two cherubim. And just as we find at Mount Sinai that it is written concerning it (Exodus 24:10): ״And they saw the Elohim of Israel,״ so too we find regarding the Tabernacle, that the Elohim of Israel was seated upon the cherubim, which is what is written concerning it (Ezekiel 10:19–20): ״And the glory of the Elohim of Israel was over them, from above… This is the living creature that I saw under the Elohim of Israel by the river Chebar, and I knew that they were cherubim.״" "<b>You shall take My terumah.</b> After he mentioned the generosity of the heart, he attributed the terumah to Himself, saying ״My terumah,״ for then His will cleaves to them, for the will of HaShem, blessed be He, is not among the narrow of eye. And furthermore, to indicate that once the donor has volunteered, it is HaShem's terumah, and if he does not give it he is punished, and if he has given it, it has already gone forth from his domain and he has nothing of it." "<b>It is possible</b> to explain that the reason he mentioned here three contributions — ויקחו לי תרומה, את תרומתי, וזאת התרומה — is that they correspond to the three contributions through which the world was elevated, namely wisdom (חכמה), understanding (תבונה), and knowledge (דעת), as it is written (Proverbs 3:19–20): ״ה' בחכמה יסד ארץ כונן שמים בתבונה, בדעתו תהומות נבקעו.״ This is alluded to in the making of the Mishkan because the Mishkan is weighed against the creation of the world, and for this reason he mentioned here three contributions corresponding to them. And corresponding to them Israel contributed three contributions in the wilderness: the first, the contribution for the Mishkan, which is the one spoken of in this parashah; the second, the contribution of shekels, which is the one spoken of in the parashah of shekels in the section Ki Tissa; the third, the contribution for the altar, which is the one spoken of in the section BeMidbar Sinai, when Israel were counted by means of shekels that each of them gave, a half-shekel, and those shekels were for the communal offerings. And of these three contributions, two are קדש and the third is חול. The two that are קדש are the contribution spoken of here and the contribution for the altar, which are קדש, for both were for the need of the Holy One, blessed be He, and for His honor; but the third, which is the contribution of shekels, was for the need of Israel and for their benefit, for through it they would gain atonement, as it is written (Exodus 30:12): ״ונתנו איש כופר נפשו.״ And corresponding to them Israel were commanded in three contributions when they entered the land: two are קדש and the third is חול, namely the contribution of חלה; and Israel were not commanded in the two to bring them outside the land but only in the Holy Land, because both are קדש; but the contribution of חלה they separate even outside the land, so that the law of חלה not be forgotten from Israel. And in order to make clear that the Mishkan is weighed against the creation of the world, the verse mentioned in it the three contributions of the world, namely what is written regarding Bezalel (Exodus 31:3): ״ואמלא אותו רוח אלהים בחכמה בתבונה ובדעת,״ and on this our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: Bezalel knew how to combine the letters through which heaven and earth were created — it is written here, ״ואמלא אותו רוח אלהים בחכמה בתבונה ובדעת,״ and it is written there (Proverbs 3:19–20): ״ה' בחכמה יסד ארץ כונן שמים בתבונה בדעתו תהומות נבקעו״ — the Sages wished to allude by this that the Mishkan is weighed against the creation of the world." "<b>And you will find</b> in the chapters of R. Eliezer: with all three of them Solomon built the Temple, for thus the verse mentioned concerning Hiram (1 Kings 7:14): ״בן אשה אלמנה הוא ממטה נפתלי ואביו איש צורי חורש נחשת וימלא את החכמה ואת התבונה ואת הדעת,״ and with all three of them it is destined to be rebuilt — this is what Solomon, peace be upon him, mentioned concerning it in the holy spirit (Proverbs 24:3–4): ״בחכמה יבנה בית ובתבונה יתכונן ובדעת חדרים ימלאו,״ and all three of them the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to give to Israel as a gift, as it is said (Proverbs 2:6): ״כי ה' יתן חכמה״ etc. — it does not say נתן but rather יתן — ״מפיו דעת ותבונה,״ and all three of them will be given to King Messiah, as it is said (Isaiah 11:2): ״ונחה עליו רוח ה' רוח חכמה ובינה״ etc. ״רוח דעת ויראת ה'.״" "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״ויקחו לי תרומה״ — since I have given you My Torah, in which it is written (Proverbs 4:2): ״כי לקח טוב נתתי לכם תורתי״ etc., bring Me a contribution. ״כי לקח טוב נתתי לכם״ — a parable: there were two merchants; one had silk and one had pepper. They exchanged: this one took pepper and that one took silk; each one lost what he had in hand. But Torah is not so: this one who studies Seder Zera'im with the one who has Seder Mo'ed in hand, and this one who studies Seder Mo'ed with the one who has Seder Zera'im in hand — each and every one gains, and what he already had in hand remains his. This is ״כי לקח טוב נתתי״ etc. There was an incident with a certain man who was on a ship with many merchants, and they kept saying to him: where is your merchandise? He said to them: my merchandise is greater than yours. They searched the entire ship and found nothing. They began laughing at him. Pirates fell upon them at sea, plundered them, and took everything that was on the ship. They came ashore and entered a city, and they had no bread to eat and no garment to wear. What did that wise man do? He went into the study hall; they treated him with honor and the great men of the city escorted him. When the merchants saw this, they came to him and implored him: we beg you, do kindness with us and plead our cause before the people of the city, so that we may be sustained and live and not die, for you have already seen what we lost on the ship. He said to them: Did I not tell you that mine is greater than yours? Yours is lost, and mine endures. And furthermore, anyone who engages in merchandise does not profit at every moment; and when he does profit, sometimes it stays in his hand and sometimes it does not stay in his hand. But Torah endures forever, in this world and in the world to come. Hence ״כי לקח טוב נתתי לכם.״" "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״ויקחו לי תרומה״ — ״ויקחו אלי״ — Knesset Yisrael, which is the contribution and which is called זאת, and therefore it says ״וזאת התרומה,״ and thus it is written (Genesis 49:28): ״וזאת אשר דבר להם אביהם,״ which I explained there, and it is written (Psalms 118:23): ״מאת ה' היתה זאת.״ And it says ״ויקחו לי״ — to unify the whole, from below upward and from above downward; and since everything depends on thought and on the intention of the heart, therefore it says: ״מאת כל איש אשר ידבנו לבו.״" "<b>And to this</b> the Midrash alludes, as they said: it is not written ״ויקחו תרומה״ here, but rather ״ויקחו לי״ — Me they take, as it were. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: take Me, that I may dwell among you. And they further said in the Midrash: ״וזאת התרומה אשר תקחו״ — Knesset Yisrael, which is the contribution, as it is said (Jeremiah 2:3): ״קדש ישראל לה' ראשית תבואתה.״" "<b>And in the book</b> Bahir you will find it stated explicitly: ״ויקחו לי תרומה״ — ״ויקחו לקדש תרומה״ — that it is יו״ד, and it is a tenth. And from where do we know that the tenth is קדש? For it is written (Leviticus 27:32): ״העשירי יהיה קדש לה׳״ — לקדש — and what is קדש? — וראשית כל בכורי כל — and it is written (Psalms 111:10): ״ראשית חכמה״, end of quotation." "<b>And that</b> which He mentioned nearby, ״ככל אשר אני מראה״ — the intent is that I am He who shows, and likewise ״כאשר הראה אותך בהר״ refers back to the word אני. And the word אני in this place is the attribute of Judgement (מדה״ד), and so we find with King David regarding the vessels of the Sanctuary, that he said (I Chronicles 28:19): ״הכל בכתב מיד ה׳ עלי השכיל כל מלאכות התבנית״ — and ״יד ה׳״ is the attribute of Judgement. And this is the matter explained here: that I am He who shows him the pattern of the Mishkan and the pattern of all its vessels." "<b>And you should know</b> that from ״ויקחו לי״ until ״וזאת התרומה״ He speaks of the supernal terumah; ״אשר תקחו מאתם זהב וכסף ונחשת״ speaks of the terumah of the Mishkan. And the explanation of the matter is: when Israel brings the terumah of the Mishkan, which is gold and silver and copper and all the rest, they shall bring with it the supernal terumah. And this is the meaning of ״ועשו לי מקדש״ — this is the terumah of the Mishkan; ״ושכנתי בתוכם״ — this is the supernal terumah. And this is the secret of the verse ״יביאה את תרומת ה׳ זהב וכסף ונחשת״, which means: together with the terumah of HaShem. Or the verse may say: ״קחו מאתכם תרומה לה׳ כל נדיב לבו יביאה את תרומת ה׳״ — ״קחו מאתכם תרומה״ — this is the supernal terumah; ״יביאה את תרומת ה׳״ — this is the terumah of the Mishkan, and therefore it says ״זהב וכסף ונחשת.״ And this is clear, that when they bring the terumah of the Mishkan they immediately bring with it the supernal terumah, and the Shekhinah dwells below among them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {3 "Comment [2] attributes the rabbinic dictum ״I toiled and found — believe…״ to רז״ל; in standard sources it appears in Megillah 6b. Comment [2] also reads כאשר לא יעשו, rendered literally.", 6 "Citation reads ירמיה כ\"ז but the verse quoted is Jeremiah 2:3; printed reference appears erroneous and is normalized in the body of comment [4]. No address added.", 9 "Printed source reads ירמיה כ\"ז; the verse cited is Jeremiah 2:3. Reference normalized; no address added.", 10 "The phrase 'וראשית כל בכורי כל' appears to be cited as a prooftext identifying קדש with ראשית, though its exact biblical source is uncertain; rendered as it stands.", 11 "Abbreviation מדה״ד resolved as מדת הדין (the attribute of Judgement) based on the context equating יד ה׳ with that attribute."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:55.275870064Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}